// @run
// @log true
// @log b
// @log b
// MISCOMPILE, pinned, AND IT IS A SILENTLY WRONG VALUE: `<` `>` `<=` `>=` over a NAMED
// literal-union alias compare INTERNED IDS, not member text. The module is valid, the
// program runs, exit 0, and the answer is wrong. This file pins the WRONG behaviour so that
// fixing it is loud (README, `xfail-*`). ALL THREE `@log`s below are the wrong answer:
// `"b" < "a"` is `false`, and sorting `["b", "a", "b"]` ascending puts `a` first — twice.
//
// THE MECHANISM, read off the disassembly of the first three lines:
//
//   type K = "a" | "b"   const x: K = "b"   const y: K = "a"   print(x < y)
//   →  (local.set $0 (i32.const 0))        ;; "b" — interned FIRST, so id 0
//      (local.set $1 (i32.const 1))        ;; "a" — interned second, id 1
//      (call $print_bool (i32.lt_s (local.get $0) (local.get $1)))
//
// A named alias `K` reps as the i32 ATOM in every scalar position (`nodeTyIsLitUnionAlias`,
// the rep #843 established), so both operands are already i32 and the relational ladder
// takes the NUMERIC arm without ever asking whether they are atoms. Ids are assigned in the
// order the members are first INTERNED — first appearance in the emitted code — so `x < y`
// here is answering "was `"b"` written before `"a"`". NOT the alias's member order, which is
// the natural guess and is wrong: `type K = "c" | "a" | "b"` sorting `["a", "b", "c"]`
// returns `a b c`, because the array literal is what interns them. Measured both ways.
//
// `==` IS NOT AFFECTED and that is why this survived: equal ids iff equal texts, so the atom
// comparison is a correct equality and the whole `eq-litunion-*` family is sound. Only the
// ORDERING operators need the operands widened to their member strings first, and they have
// no twin of the widen `==` never needed.
//
// THE INLINE SPELLING IS NOT A WORKAROUND, and the third row proves it. `"a" | "b"` written
// inline SOFTENS to a string ref in a scalar position (`ctxKeepsLitUnion` at `RC_ROOT`), so
// `<` dispatches to the string comparison and answers on the TEXT — but only while no
// same-layout ALIAS is declared anywhere in the module. `type K = "a" | "b"` above is enough,
// and it does not have to be USED: the inline spelling registers as that alias (the
// same-layout rule `literal-unions/error-same-layout-litunion-arg.vl` pins) and orders by id
// too. Measured at three members, where the difference is unmistakable — one UNUSED
// `type K = "a" | "b" | "c"` line flips `["c", "a", "b"].sorted(<)` from `a b c` to `c a b`.
//
// So the CORRECT inline row cannot live in this file, and it is in
// `tests/cases/std/array-sorted.vl`, which declares no alias. The pair is the whole
// statement of the defect: identical `sorted` calls over identically-spelled element types,
// one file apart, disagreeing because of a type declaration neither call mentions.
//
// THE WORKAROUND THAT DOES WORK, measured with `type K` declared: copy the elements into a
// `string[]` and sort that — `for k in ks { strs.push(k) }` then `strs.sorted(<)` returns
// `a b c`. The push crosses the atom→string widen, so the comparison is on text again.
//
// THE CONSEQUENCE, and how it was found: `std:array`'s `sorted` over a `K[]` sorts by
// interned id — for a list already in first-appearance order it is a no-op that looks like a
// sort. The inline row in `array-sorted.vl` sorts the same three values correctly, so the
// alias receiver is now the broken half of a pair whose INLINE half was the broken one until
// the element-widen family closed
// (`soundness/generic-litunion-element-bind-and-store.vl`).
//
// WHEN IT IS FIXED all three `@log`s become `false`, `a`, `a`, and the prefix goes away.
import { sorted } from "std:array"

type K = "a" | "b"

const x: K = "b"
const y: K = "a"
print(x < y)

const ks: K[] = ["b", "a", "b"]
print(ks.sorted((p: K, q: K) => p < q)[0])

const inl: ("a" | "b")[] = ["b", "a", "b"]
print(inl.sorted((p: "a" | "b", q: "a" | "b") => p < q)[0])
